<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kewu Plain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prambanan Plain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kewu Plain, also called the Prambanan Plain, is a fertile area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, on the southern slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-merapi">Mount Merapi</a> near Yogyakarta. In the eighth and ninth centuries it was a heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang</a> kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra dynasty</a>, and it holds a dense group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist temples, including Prambanan, Sewu and Plaosan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kewu-plain.jpg" alt="Kewu Plain" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BPCB Jawa Tengah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_Sajiwan-Prambanan_temples.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
